      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Postoperative expectations after stapled hemorrhoidopexy are still being clarified. Our purpose was to evaluate how outcome is affected by staple line height (SLH) above the dentate line and specimen histology."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A prospective database identified demographics, SLH, histology, narcotic use, return to work, and resolution or recurrence of preoperative symptoms data for analysis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "One hundred five patients were analyzed; median age was 49 years. Median RTW and narcotic use were 9 and 4 days, respectively. Patients with squamous epithelium-containing specimens had longer narcotic use (P = .038), whereas patients with SLH >20 mm had shorter narcotic use (P = .021). Preoperative pain and bleeding resolved more frequently with SLH >20 mm (P = .036) and less frequently with SLH >40 mm (P = .032). Patients with poor sphincter tone were more likely to have SLH >20 mm (P = .044). Postoperative symptoms recurred more frequently in patients with SLH >40 mm (P = .001)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Hemorrhoidopexy SLH and histology can impact postoperative outcomes. SLH should be >20 mm yet < or =40 mm above the dentate, avoiding squamous epithelium."
        }
      ]},
